 32nds. The first half of bar 40 must be on the previous line. The space only fits about four groups; bar 44, written out in full later on this line, takes twice the width.\\n\\nNext stretch: bar 41. Grace notes over 16th + two 32nds, twice. Then two 8ths (A4 G#4), a dotted figure (A4 16th., G4 32nd), and the G4 quarter. The bar ends with two 16ths (G5 F5), marked p.\\n\\nAfter the next barline: bar 42. Grace F5, E5 8th, E5 F5 16ths, natural G5 8ths, a dotted G5 16th figure, E5 8th, G5 F5 16ths.\\n\\nThen bar 43: the same figure, ending in a 32nd group (G5 F5 G5 E5) with 'cres.' below.\\n\\nThen bar 44: a long run of dense 32nd arpeggio groups. They climb D, E, F, then natural G, and fall back. The bar closes with the slurred group and the grace-note group (C6 grace, B5, A#5 B5; the sharp is visible).\\n\\nAfter the last barline: bar 45 begins. Natural A5 grace, G5 16th and two 32nds, then the second grace group (A5, G5, F5 E5). The line ends partway through bar 45; the next line finishes it.\\n\\nNothing contradicts the edition. Bar 40 is only partly on this line, but it still counts as the line's.\",\"right\":true,\"insights\":\"The middle bars (41–43) were easy to place. Their grace-note figures, natural signs, the p and the 'cres.' line up one-to-one with the edition. The hard parts were the two ends. The 32nd arpeggio groups are dense blocks of beams whose noteheads are hard to read at this resolution, and bar 40 is split across lines. Knowing where the previous line ends, or having a higher-resolution crop of the opening groups, would settle where bar 40 is cut.\"}","structured_output":{"working":"Start (after clef and three sharps, up to the first barline): four beamed groups of 32nds. The third has a slur, and the last has a grace note on top followed by a 16th and two 32nds. This is the tail of bar 40: the C and B arpeggio groups, then A4 A5 G5 A5, then grace B5, A5 16th, G5 F5 32nds. The first half of bar 40 must be on the previous line. The space only fits about four groups; bar 44, written out in full later on this line, takes twice the width.\n\nNext stretch: bar 41. Grace notes over 16th + two 32nds, twice. Then two 8ths (A4 G#4), a dotted figure (A4 16th., G4 32nd), and the G4 quarter. The bar ends with two 16ths (G5 F5), marked p.\n\nAfter the next barline: bar 42. Grace F5, E5 8th, E5 F5 16ths, natural G5 8ths, a dotted G5 16th figure, E5 8th, G5 F5 16ths.\n\nThen bar 43: the same figure, ending in a 32nd group (G5 F5 G5 E5) with 'cres.' below.\n\nThen bar 44: a long run of dense 32nd arpeggio groups. They climb D, E, F, then natural G, and fall back. The bar closes with the slurred group and the grace-note group (C6 grace, B5, A#5 B5; the sharp is visible).\n\nAfter the last barline: bar 45 begins. Natural A5 grace, G5 16th and two 32nds, then the second grace group (A5, G5, F5 E5). The line ends partway through bar 45; the next line finishes it.\n\nNothing contradicts the edition. Bar 40 is only partly on this line, but it still counts as the line's.","right":true,"insights":"The middle bars (41–43) were easy to place. Their grace-note figures, natural signs, the p and the 'cres.' line up one-to-one with the edition. The hard parts were the two ends. The 32nd arpeggio groups are dense blocks of beams whose noteheads are hard to read at this resolution, and bar 40 is split across lines. Knowing where the previous line ends, or having a higher-resolution crop of the opening groups, would settle where bar 40 is cut."},"ttft_ms":38955,"type":"result","duration_ms":47605,"uuid":"727ec631-799b-4271-b768-86c5bc3b85fc","ttft_stream_ms":1647,"time_to_request_ms":512,"first_content_frame_ms":1647,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":4056,"cacheReadInputTokens":523,"cacheCreationInputTokens":6194,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.13078459999999997,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":3227,"cacheCreationEphe